        fp: "A general contractor was preparing a formal bid to construct an athletic complex for a private university. Bids were due at the university's procurement office by 2:00 PM on Friday. On Thursday afternoon, an electrical subcontractor submitted a signed written quote to the general contractor: 'We offer to perform the complete electrical subcontract scope on the university athletic complex project according to plans and specifications for $420,000. This quote shall remain firm and open for 30 days.' The general contractor used the subcontractor's $420,000 bid figure in compiling its master prime bid, which was timely submitted at 1:30 PM on Friday. At 1:45 PM on Friday, before prime bids were unsealed, the subcontractor telephoned the general contractor and stated: 'We made an arithmetic error omitting the stadium lighting controls; our price should have been $510,000. We hereby revoke our $420,000 quote.' The university awarded the prime contract to the general contractor on the following Monday. The general contractor immediately sent written notice to the subcontractor accepting the $420,000 bid. The subcontractor refused to perform for less than $510,000. The general contractor was forced to hire the next lowest competitive electrical subcontractor for $495,000.",
