        fp: "A manufacturer of high-end home audio components designed and marketed a compact desktop vacuum-tube amplifier. The amplifier was engineered with exposed glass vacuum tubes operating at 300 volts direct current (DC). The manufacturer included a detachable metal mesh cage that could be clipped over the tubes, and printed a warning on the bottom chassis: 'WARNING: High Voltage. Install protective mesh cage before operation.' An audiophile purchased the amplifier from an authorized retail dealer. The audiophile preferred the visual aesthetic of the glowing glass tubes, intentionally left the protective mesh cage in the packaging box, and operated the amplifier exposed on an open credenza in his living room. The audiophile hosted a dinner party. A social guest, admiring the glowing tubes, reached forward to touch the glass. The guest's finger brushed an exposed electrical contact pin at the base of a power tube, delivering a 300-volt electric shock that caused severe electrical burns and ventricular cardiac arrest. The guest brought a strict products liability action against the amplifier manufacturer, alleging a design defect. The manufacturer proved that the amplifier was safe when operated with the supplied mesh cage, and that the audiophile's removal of the cage was a product alteration and misuse.",
